def max_product(arr):
    if len(arr) < 2:
        return arr[0]

    max1 = max2 = min1 = min2 = arr[0]

    for num in arr[1:]:
        if num > max1:
            max2, max1 = max1, num
        elif num > max2:
            max2 = num

        if num < min1:
            min2, min1 = min1, num
        elif num < min2:
            min2 = num

    return max(max1 * max2, min1 * min2)
```

This solution uses two variables to keep track of the maximum and minimum values seen so far as it iterates through the array. It also keeps track of the second largest and smallest values separately. This approach ensures that we only need to traverse the array once, resulting in a time complexity of O(n). The space complexity is O(1), as we are using a constant amount of extra space regardless of the input size.

