def ternary_search(arr, to_find):
    left, right = 0, len(arr) - 1

    while left <= right:
        mid1 = left + (right - left) // 3
        mid2 = right - (right - left) // 3

        if arr[mid1] == to_find:
            return mid1
        elif arr[mid2] == to_find:
            return mid2
        elif to_find < arr[mid1]:
            right = mid1 - 1
        else:
            if to_find > arr[mid2]:
                left = mid2 + 1
            else:
                left, right = mid1 + 1, mid2 - 1

    return -1
```
This function implements the Ternary Search algorithm in Python. It first checks if the target value is present at any of the three middle points of the array. If it is, the index of that point is returned. Otherwise, it narrows down the search range by eliminating half of the remaining elements based on whether the target value is less than or greater than the middle values. The process continues until the target value is found or the search range is exhausted.

